var e=`# CHAPTER 10 — The Green Hair

A week later I was in a meeting with all my lieutenants from the neighborhood.

We were dealing with a pain in the ass. Some cops — the kind we paid to look the other way — had decided the price of turning their heads had gone up.

That's more dangerous than it looks. You let one guy raise his price just because he felt like it, the next month all the others raise theirs too, and then there's no cash box that can hold. We were going to need a show of force. Something to make those guys understand the payment was fair and was going to stay fair.

In the middle of that, a guy walks into the room and calls me.

— There's a kid downstairs looking for you. By name.

---

I went out in a bad mood. It was an important meeting.

Downstairs there was a kid the same age as last week's. Only this one was Black — Black like me — with real white teeth, and hair dyed green so badly it was a crime.

And he was looking for me. By my name.

I didn't get where these kids were coming from. Or how this one had managed to climb the whole Hill without anybody stopping him.

— What's up — I said. — That's me.

He broke into a grin as big as his face.

— Oh, cool. So you're Alan.

And he jumped right in trying to punch me in the face. A goofy kind of jump, telegraphed, the move of somebody who saw it in a fight once and never did it.

He took a kick to the stomach and went backwards. Laughed from the ground, sitting there, one hand on his belly.

— Whoa. — He looked up at me, still laughing. — So you really are the champ, huh? The one they talk about. — He got up pointing his finger at me like he'd snapped a picture. — I'm gonna call you Champ.

The name stuck. Years later those two still use it — [main story, ch. 3](/historias/lutas-de-ilusao/capitulo-03).

Until that day nobody had called me that. It was a seven-year-old kid, sitting on the ground, with my footprint on his stomach, who gave me the name the rest of Marélia was going to use forever.

---

I didn't understand a thing about my own life anymore.

What kind of crazy neighborhood was this. What kind of place was this where street kids showed up every week just to get beat up by me.

This one's style was different from the other one's. Sloppy. Show-off. Like he was playing around, relaxed, with nothing at stake. But under the sloppiness he had the same thing the Kid had. Instinct. Clear instinct of somebody who knew how to fight.

And, again, a kid half my age.

---

This time I started out serious. I'd learned from the first one.

I kept hitting. A punch here, a punch there, no ceremony, because I had a meeting waiting.

And things started changing again — only the opposite of the other one.

The Kid had gone wild when he took a beating. This one got precise. Every shot he took, his next one came out cleaner, more on target. Some of them started landing on me.

But he was getting weaker. I could feel it. The difference is he didn't let it show. To anybody watching from outside, it looked like the kid was getting better. Getting stronger with every hit.

Damn green hair. Gave me a hard time.

---

The other one refused to fall. This one, when he hit his limit, dropped his arms.

— Stop. Stop, stop. I give up.

He knew when to call it. Lost fair and square. It was actually funny.

— I'd rather walk out of here than end up laid out on the ground. Thanks.

— Get lost, kid. And don't come back here.

— Nah. — He wiped the blood from his nose with the back of his hand, still smiling. — I'm coming back. And next time I'm gonna beat you.

---

One kid who wouldn't fall. Another who'd accept losing, but only so he could come back the next day.

I started seeing a pattern.

The two of them had the same thing inside. Instinct — the thing you can't buy with time.

I could recruit both of them. I should have. But once again I was buried in meetings, and I let it slide.
`;export{e as default};
